OncePerRequestFilter secondFilter() {
    return logging("second filter");
}

steps.body:
When a user writes in to say their booking failed at about ten past two, you need to find their request among thousands of log lines. A **request id** makes that quick. It's a unique label given to each request, written into every log line the request causes, and sent back to the client in a header. The client can show it on an error screen, and support can search the logs for it. When Roombook calls another service later in the course, it passes the id along, so one id follows the request through every system it touches. That's why it's also called a **correlation id**.

## Read it or make it

The id travels in the `X-Request-Id` header. When a request already has one, from a proxy in front of the API or a client that made its own, Roombook keeps it, so the id matches across systems. Otherwise, it makes a new random one. Either way, the id goes back on the response.

A header is input from outside, so check it before you trust it. Roombook keeps an incoming id only if it's 1 to 64 letters, digits, dots, dashes, and underscores. Anything else is replaced with a fresh id: a header of ten thousand characters, or one with a line break meant to forge an extra line in your logs.

## Request-scoped storage

Code further in needs the id: handlers that log, the error handler, and calls to other services. Passing it as an argument through every function would be clumsy, and a global variable would be wrong, since a server works on many requests at once and a global would mix them up. Every framework has somewhere to keep values for one request only, called **request-scoped storage**.

In Spring, the request id goes in the **MDC**, the Mapped Diagnostic Context of SLF4J, the logging library Spring uses. The MDC is a small map that belongs to the current thread, and Tomcat runs each request on its own thread, so for the length of a request, the map belongs to that request. Here is the filter, in `common/RequestIdFilter.java`:

```java
@Component
@Order(Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE)
public class RequestIdFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(RequestIdFilter.class);
    private static final Pattern SAFE_ID = Pattern.compile("[A-Za-z0-9._-]{1,64}");

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response,
            FilterChain chain) throws ServletException, IOException {
        String incoming = request.getHeader("X-Request-Id");
        String requestId = incoming != null && SAFE_ID.matcher(incoming).matches()
                ? incoming
                : UUID.randomUUID().toString();
        MDC.put("requestId", requestId);
        response.setHeader("X-Request-Id", requestId);
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        try {
            chain.doFilter(request, response);
        } finally {
            double ms = Math.round((System.nanoTime() - start) / 100_000.0) / 10.0;
            log.info("{} {} {} {}ms", request.getMethod(), request.getRequestURI(),
                    response.getStatus(), ms);
            MDC.remove("requestId");
        }
    }
}
```

`UUID.randomUUID()` makes a **UUID**, a 128-bit random id written as 36 characters, which is unique enough that two requests will never share one. Any code can read it back with `MDC.get("requestId")`, and the logging pattern can print it on every line. The `MDC.remove` in `finally` isn't tidiness: Tomcat reuses its threads, and without it, the next request on this thread would start out with this one's id.

## Timing the whole request

The filter that sets the id also times the request, since it's the outermost layer. The `finally` writes the log line even when an exception escapes, and by then the status is final. One setting in `src/main/resources/application.yaml` puts the id on every log line, in brackets:

```yaml
logging:
  pattern:
    console: '%d{HH:mm:ss.SSS} %-5level [%X{requestId:-}] %logger{24} - %msg%n'
```

`%X{requestId:-}` prints the MDC's `requestId`, or nothing when there isn't one. Two requests through the app print:

```text
23:50:40.748 INFO  [bebdf9e4-35e6-4a03-afb5-b85465b4faa0] d.h.r.c.RequestIdFilter - GET /v1/rooms 200 26.6ms
23:50:40.757 INFO  [demo-42] d.h.r.c.RequestIdFilter - GET /v1/rooms 200 0.7ms
```

## Answering early

A middleware can also decide that a request goes no further. Roombook has a read-only mode for maintenance, like the few minutes while its database moves to a new server. Reads keep working, and anything that would change data gets `503 Service Unavailable`, with a `Retry-After` header that says how many seconds to wait before trying again. Because it's a middleware, it covers every write route at once, including ones added next year.

Here is the read-only switch, in `common/ReadOnlyFilter.java`. It only exists when the setting `roombook.read-only` is `true`, thanks to `@ConditionalOnBooleanProperty`, and for anything but a safe method, it writes the response itself and never calls the chain:

```java
@Component
@Order(10)
@ConditionalOnBooleanProperty("roombook.read-only")
public class ReadOnlyFilter extends OncePerRequestFilter {

    private static final Set<String> SAFE_METHODS = Set.of("GET", "HEAD", "OPTIONS");

    @Override
    protected void doFilterInternal(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response,
            FilterChain chain) throws ServletException, IOException {
        if (SAFE_METHODS.contains(request.getMethod())) {
            chain.doFilter(request, response);
            return;
        }
        response.setStatus(503);
        response.setHeader("Retry-After", "600");
        response.setContentType("application/json");
        response.setCharacterEncoding(StandardCharsets.UTF_8);
        response.getWriter().write("{\"status\":503,\"title\":\"Roombook is read-only for maintenance\"}");
    }
}
```

A filter works below Spring MVC, so there's no automatic JSON here: you write the body yourself. Set the character encoding before you call `getWriter()`, or the Servlet standard's old default, ISO-8859-1, goes in the header.

Handlers fail. A database goes away, a bug reads a field of something that isn't there, a library throws an error nobody expected. Without a plan, what the client gets depends on where it failed and on the framework's defaults. It might be an HTML error page from a JSON API, or a **stack trace**, the list of function calls that led to the error, which tells a stranger which libraries and files you use. It might be a connection that simply closes.

Error-handling middleware is the plan. It's one place, at the edge of the pipeline, that every unhandled error reaches, and that turns each one into a response you chose. It has three jobs:

1. **Log the details for yourself**: the error, its stack trace, and the request id, so you can find and fix it.
2. **Answer the client safely**: a 500 with a short, generic message and the request id, and nothing about your code. The client can't fix a bug in the server, and the details would only help someone looking for one.
3. **Answer unknown paths with a 404** in the same JSON shape, instead of the framework's default page.

## Controller advice in Spring

In Spring, exceptions thrown by controllers go to **controller advice**: a class marked `@RestControllerAdvice` whose methods, marked `@ExceptionHandler`, say which exceptions they handle. Here is Roombook's first version, in `common/ApiExceptionHandler.java`:

```java
@RestControllerAdvice
class ApiExceptionHandler extends ResponseEntityExceptionHandler {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(ApiExceptionHandler.class);

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> unexpected(Exception ex) {
        log.error("unhandled error", ex);
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR).body(Map.of(
                "status", 500,
                "title", "Internal Server Error",
                "requestId", MDC.get("requestId")));
    }
}
```

Extending `ResponseEntityExceptionHandler` matters more than it looks. Spring MVC throws its own exceptions for requests it can't serve: `NoResourceFoundException` when no controller matches the path, others for a wrong method, a wrong content type, or a body that isn't JSON. That base class already has a handler for each, with the right status: 404, 405, 415, 400. When several handlers could take an exception, Spring picks the one for the closest type, so those specific handlers win and yours only gets the rest. Without the base class, `@ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)` would also catch a wrong method and answer 500 instead of 405.

`log.error` with the exception as the last argument writes the stack trace, and the MDC from part 3 puts the request id on that line. The advice only sees exceptions from inside the DispatcherServlet. One thrown in a filter goes past it, to Spring Boot's fallback error page at `/error`, so filters should catch their own.

The next lesson, on validation and errors, turns these answers into the standard error format every Roombook error uses, and teaches the same handler to answer 400, 409, and 422 when a request breaks the rules. For now, it covers the two cases every API has from its first day: a path that doesn't exist, and a bug.

Filters and controller advice have their places in Spring's pipeline, as parts 2 to 4 showed. The third hook, the **interceptor**, sits between them, and Spring Boot sets up a handful of filters and switches for you, so you rarely write those yourself.

## Interceptors

A `HandlerInterceptor` runs inside the DispatcherServlet, after it has picked the controller method, so unlike a filter, it knows which method is about to run. It has three methods, all optional:

- `preHandle` runs before the controller. Return `false` and the controller doesn't run, which is the interceptor's way to answer early.
- `postHandle` runs after the controller returns, if it didn't throw.
- `afterCompletion` runs at the very end, even after an exception, like a `finally`.

Here is the one the order example used, in `common/HandlerTimingInterceptor.java`, which logs the controller method each request reached:

```java
@Component
class HandlerTimingInterceptor implements HandlerInterceptor {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(HandlerTimingInterceptor.class);

    @Override
    public boolean preHandle(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response, Object handler) {
        if (handler instanceof HandlerMethod method) {
            log.info("handled by {}.{}", method.getBeanType().getSimpleName(), method.getMethod().getName());
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

Interceptors aren't found automatically like filters. You register them, with their paths, in a `WebMvcConfigurer`, which is also where Spring MVC's CORS rules go:

```java
@Configuration
class WebConfig implements WebMvcConfigurer {

    private final HandlerTimingInterceptor timing;

    WebConfig(HandlerTimingInterceptor timing) {
        this.timing = timing;
    }

    @Override
    public void addInterceptors(InterceptorRegistry registry) {
        registry.addInterceptor(timing).addPathPatterns("/v1/**");
    }

    @Override
    public void addCorsMappings(CorsRegistry registry) {
        registry.addMapping("/v1/**")
                .allowedOrigins("http://localhost:5173")
                .allowedMethods("GET", "POST", "PUT", "PATCH", "DELETE")
                .exposedHeaders("X-Request-Id", "Location", "ETag");
    }
}
```

The `WebConfig` gets the interceptor through its constructor: Spring creates the interceptor bean and passes it in, which the lesson on layers explains.

## What Spring Boot sets up for you

- A `CharacterEncodingFilter` that reads request bodies as UTF-8, and a few more filters at the start of the chain.
- Compression, with `server.compression.enabled: true` in `application.yaml`.
- `ShallowEtagHeaderFilter`, if you register it, adds an `ETag` to every response from a hash of its body.
- Security headers come with Spring Security, which the authentication lesson adds along with its own filter chain, and the security lesson tunes them.
- Health checks and metrics come with Spring Boot Actuator, which the first project already has, and which the reliability and deployment lessons put to work.

## Which hook to use

| Hook | Runs | Knows the controller method | Good for |
|---|---|---|---|
| Filter | Around everything, before Spring MVC | No | Request ids, logging, security, anything every request needs |
| Interceptor | Inside the DispatcherServlet, around the controller | Yes | Checks or logs tied to a controller or its annotations |
| Controller advice | When a controller throws | Yes | Turning exceptions into responses |

When a job must happen for every request, including ones no controller matches, it's a filter. When it depends on which controller runs, it's an interceptor. When it turns an exception into an answer, it's advice, and the validation lesson grows Roombook's into the one place every error goes.
